13822747221b0183521e197814b19ab42ee60ad3Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T17:24:13-04:002026-09-10T17:24:13-04:00Keep the collapsed ascription's id so its probe samples the value

Collapsing a pair of nested ascriptions minted the node with a fresh id,
so no evaluation ever ran under the inner ascription's id and the
evaluator could not open an observation span for it. The outermost
ascription of a chain was the only level that sampled at all; the others
sampled a value minted at collapse time, i.e. the operand unevaluated,
with the whole environment substituted into it.

Stamping the collapsed node with e's id -- what ID PRESERVATION FOR
PROBES at the top of this file already prescribes -- gives every level a
span that closes on the final value, so the collapse-time minting is no
longer needed. Collapsing is otherwise unchanged: same guard, same meet,
same recursion, so chains are still eliminated before the operand runs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

5e832d1f7f2d92faa46226198a018e2840d91c2dAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T17:24:13-04:002026-09-10T17:24:13-04:00Clear use_mode before typing a probe sample

A sample is an already-elaborated runtime value, but type_of_sample ran
statics on it in the source's context, whose use_mode is still set. That
re-runs Operators.replace_literal over the value, so inside `use Nat` an
Int sample was reported as Nat. Ctx.re documents use_mode as None once
elaboration has occurred, and CachedStatics passes ctx_init(None) for
dynamic terms for the same reason.

