3c8cf8c3d91dbae0a6d7451b7d4b52ce75f76624Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T10:26:17-04:002026-09-10T10:26:17-04:00Don't offer the statics projector on type positions

`init` accepted `Typ(_)`, but nothing downstream can serve it: `Info.typ`
carries no expected or self type, and types are not evaluated, so all three
modes render `?` forever. The `self_ty` case that e4e1a7cb80 added alongside
this was lost in a later dev merge when `Info.typ`'s field was renamed
`term` -> `user_term`, and restoring it would not help -- `expected_ty` is
still None, so the "no expectation, show self instead" arm never fires and
`Self` stays unreachable.

Showing a type back to itself is redundant anyway; the useful version would
show it alias-expanded, which is a different feature.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

f8d7c18a9f01b9d40029dc6ef98dab4591bc4eceAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T10:26:17-04:002026-09-10T10:26:17-04:00Don't collect ascription samples during pattern matching

`matches_inner` and `match_pattern` threaded the real targets into
`Ascriptions.transition_multiple`, which walks the writer and builds a sample
list, and then bound it to `_samples` and dropped it -- so the collection work
was wasted and a reader could not tell whether dropping it was deliberate.

Dropping is deliberate: the scrutinee reaches matching already final, so any
probe under its ascriptions has fired, at the call stack where evaluation
actually passed through it. Recording again here would double-count the same
values under whatever stack the match happens to run on. Passing
`Sample.no_targets` says that, and matches what Unboxing already does.

`match_pattern`'s `~targets` was only there to feed that call, so it goes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

721d54fc948d64716304e8b8b6badac804fb644cAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T10:08:24-04:002026-09-10T10:08:24-04:00Stop Typ.join looping on cyclic type aliases

Same defect as the one just fixed in Typ.diff, in the function this branch
adds: both Var cases expand aliases through the context with no record of what
they have already expanded, so `type A = B in type B = A` recurses until the
stack goes. join runs in the statics pass, via
LiveTyping.extend_ctx_with_instantiations, so the symptom is a crash on every
keystroke rather than a bad rendering.

A cyclic alias has no join, which is what the existing unexpandable-alias
branch already returns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

cc542d82fbd9c7961dcef978ea4e5930de0e34d4Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T10:08:02-04:002026-09-10T10:08:02-04:00Merge dyntype-proj into dynamic_statics

Three resolutions worth noting:

- TypeProj's `get_dynamic_typ` became `get_dynamic_segment`, which renders and
  marks in one place. This branch's pin filtering had been added to the old
  function; it now runs on the samples before they reach
  displayed_segment_and_marks, so both survive.
- CursorInspector called `PadIds.compute_dynamic_ids`, and dyntype-proj deleted
  PadIds. Git merged that file without a conflict -- deleted callee, kept
  caller -- so it only showed up as `Unbound module`. The marking core is
  extracted here as `DynamicTypInfer.segment_and_marks`, taking a type pair,
  with displayed_segment_and_marks the sample-inferring wrapper over it; the
  inspector calls the former and renders the segment it returns. Rendering the
  type again there would re-normalize it and produce a segment the marks do
  not describe.

  The sentinel replacement travels into segment_and_marks with the rest of the
  body, which is what the inspector needs: it is handed a live-typing
  elab_syn_ty, and statics builds those with Typ.temp too. Without it the
  inspector marked `Int` and the enclosing parens on a type refined to
  `(Int, Bool)`, leaving `Bool` plain.
- Test_DynamicTypInfer's scoping and marking suites both landed just above
  `tests`; both are kept.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

38824b83cdeebdf6001f5738da3989bfc0516af5Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T09:22:11-04:002026-09-10T10:05:51-04:00Give the inferred dynamic type ids that name its own tokens

Statics builds the types it infers with `Typ.temp`, which stamps every node
with the `Id.invalid` sentinel. Rendered as-is the marks collapsed to that one
id, and `uniquify_repeated_tiles` minted fresh ids for every tile after the
first -- so all but one token was in no type and could not be marked. Worse,
whichever tile the renderer happened to emit first kept the sentinel, so on a
partially-known type the mark landed on a token statics had supplied:
`(fun x -> ^^statics((1, x)))(true)` coloured `Int` instead of `Bool`. It also
made `diff`'s wrapped_replaced test fire on any node sharing the sentinel with
a replaced one, marking the enclosing parens too. `Typ.replace_temp` already
existed for exactly this and had no callers.

The replacement goes where a type's ids become the ids of rendered tokens,
not where the type is inferred -- statics-built types reach this point from
more than one direction.

Also: `necessary_ids` had no `Sig` case, so an n-item signature's separators
were minted during rendering and could never be marked. It is `max(1, n)`,
the same as `Sum` -- rep_id for the braces plus one per `;`.

`Segment.tile_ids` is extracted next to `Segment.ids` rather than hand-rolled
a third time; it is the set of ids Code.re can actually class, since Grout and
Secondary carry none.

Tests go through `displayed_segment_and_marks` with `ProjectorInfo.utility` --
the composition the projector runs. A generator cannot find this: every
`QCheck_Util.arb_typ` node gets a distinct id, which is the one precondition
production violated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

6b3bdfe0a584decc38e51fb849643ecf374a139fAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-10T09:21:58-04:002026-09-10T09:21:58-04:00Fix the id set Typ.diff returns, and make it terminate

Three defects, each found by rendering the dynamic type of a real probe
rather than a generated one:

- A cyclic alias chain (`type A = B in type B = A`) expanded forever.
  `expanded_aliases` records the names expanded on the current chain of Var
  lookups; every other case resets it, since a structural descent consumes a
  constructor from the finite left type.
- `(_, Var(name))` returned ids from the context's alias expansion, which are
  never in the rendered segment: an alias renders as one token carrying the
  Var node's own id. The expansion now only decides whether the alias
  differs. This also stops `Ctx.lookup_alias`'s freshly-minted
  `Unknown(Hole(Invalid(name)))` for unbound names from leaking ids.
- `(Parens(t1), Parens(t2))` dropped the dynamic parens' ids unconditionally,
  skipping the `wrapped_replaced` test that `(_, Parens(t2))` exists to apply.
  Deleting the case is the fix; both-parenthesized now falls through to it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQDwH6tMjSMGSSykhjgQUP

e644dcb91b98d6c59add8cf70b05e0d7885aa0f7disconcision2026-09-09T17:47:24-07:002026-09-09T17:47:24-07:00Release the one-row depth-below clamp for rich probe chips

In Many mode a depth-below sample is clamped to a one-row height so its
stacked shadow + 3px translate fit inside a text line. Rich chips (card
hands, ~3 rows) were clamped too: the sample-group centers its items, so
the clamped box sat a third of the way down the group and the card
overflowing from its top landed a full row below the unclamped
depth-same/above neighbours — and jumped as arrow keys moved the focus.
Rich chips now keep their natural height; the translate stagger stays.

