6d626a497448f436bfc749730b0c664802de2f93Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-08T11:47:53-04:002026-09-09T12:31:32-04:00Say what each check asserts instead of restating its value

Several check messages were the expected value written twice -- `"c"` as the
message for a check whose expected value was `"c"` -- and in the breadcrumb
cases the message was a bare slide name sitting next to a call that takes a
slide name, so it read like an input rather than a label. Messages now name
the operation and its subject: "leaf of a / b / c", "position of
Basics / Holes", "breadcrumb for Tables / Filtering".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

03c1b8b07c6eb02f9d44185d47a2cc705759b662Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-08T11:42:21-04:002026-09-09T12:31:32-04:00Name what SlidePath's ints mean, and test in slide names

folder_position returned (int, int), which said nothing about which int was
which. It now returns a record:

    type folder_position = {
      index_in_folder: int,
      folder_size: int,
    };

step_in_folder still returns an int, but its comment now says what that int
is: a position in the same list it was given.

`separator` is built from `separator_char` rather than being written out a
second time, so the two cannot drift.

The example space in the .rei no longer reuses a folder name as a slide name
-- "Tables / Tables" became "Tables / Filtering" -- which makes the
breadcrumb example readable, since the two crumbs there now differ.

Shortened two comments in TutorialsMode that were saying more than they
needed to.

Tests act on slides by name instead of by index. A space is a list of names,
and helpers over it (`position_of`, `step_from`, `crumbs_of`, `jumps_of`)
take a name and, where the result is a slide, report a name back:

    check(string, "Basics / Functions forward stays put",
          "Basics / Functions", step_from(~by=1, "Basics / Functions"))

So a failure names the slides involved rather than printing bare indices, and
a mistyped slide fails loudly in `index_of` instead of silently passing. The
index bindings this replaces (`basics_holes` and friends) are gone, along
with the last uses of "Tables / Tables" and "Rich Probes / Rich Probes" as
fixture names.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

23f221e3c18a2fa2ec714bc713da4f2f0d656b2fAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-08T11:28:46-04:002026-09-09T12:31:32-04:00Give SlidePath an explicit API and address review

Adds SlidePath.rei. The type is abstract, so a path only exists by parsing a
name or building one from parts, both of which normalize -- which closes a
hole: Init.re was constructing `{folders: ["Derivations"], leaf: title}`
directly, so nothing stopped a segment holding a separator from producing a
path that would not render back to its own name. It now calls `mk`. Kept
internal: `separator`, `separator_char`, `same_folder` and `folder_indices`,
all pure implementation of the folder navigation. `depth` is deleted; it had
no caller but its own test.

Doc comments move to the .rei, each with a minimal example, and the nav
operations share one four-slide example space declared once in the header.
They describe what a path is rather than naming current call sites, and the
.re keeps only implementation notes so the contract is not written twice.

Other review fixes:

- `ListUtil.split_last` replaces the `List.rev`-and-match in `of_string`,
  which also removes its impossible empty-list branch.
- The separator is no longer written in two places: `separator` and
  `separator_char` sit together, one joined by `to_string` and one split on by
  `of_string`.
- No polymorphic equality: `List.equal(String.equal, ...)` in `same_folder`
  and in `breadcrumb`'s prefix comparison, `String.equal` in its dedup, and
  `equal` is derived rather than hand-written (`ppx_deriving.eq` added to the
  web library).
- `folder` returns `option(string)`, with None for a path that has no folder
  segment, rather than an empty-string sentinel.
- `step_in_folder`'s comment said "The index `by` steps from `current`", which
  parsed as nonsense; it now says what it does.

Tests: one suite per public function, and every navigation case names the
path it starts from ("Basics / Functions stays put") instead of a bare index,
with the example space's positions bound to names. Test_Tutorial gains an
id-uniqueness ratchet, which is what the README's claim that identity comes
from the id actually rests on -- the per-lesson store key is the id, so two
lessons sharing one would share saved work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

ba599d899b4ea867f178e30d8b0dabfd9fd5d71fAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-08T11:14:52-04:002026-09-09T12:31:32-04:00Port three table-study tasks into the Tables folder

Adds the Gradebook Midterm Mean, Gradebook Overall Grade, and Gradebook Tidy
Term tasks from the `tables-study` branch as lessons 26-28, bringing Tables to
six. They keep their original ids, so they are the same lessons the study ran;
the "Task 2: " prefix on Midterm Mean is dropped, since that ordinal came from
the study's positional title rewriting rather than from the lesson.

On tables-study these were `Ta_*.ml` zipper dumps, 0.5-0.9 MB each. Rather than
hand-translate them they were adapted to the current types -- the drift was two
things per file, `selection` gaining `anchor_caret`/`smart_rounded` and
`refractors` dropping `autos`/`sample_cursor` with `manuals` unchanged --
compiled in alongside `TaskRefDocs`, then run through `./hazel tutorial-decode`,
which also expanded each task's composed `TaskRefDocs` sections into a literal
`@reference`. None of that scaffolding remains in the tree.

Two content fixes on top of the decode:

- Midterm Mean's table held 20 literals like `85.000000`. Past six significant
  digits a slide falls onto the quadratic parser, with slowness as the only
  symptom, so these are normalized to `85.0` -- value-identical, and the form
  every other lesson uses.
- Its hidden tests did not round-trip: an implicit `¿` hole collapses to
  nothing before a `,` inside a labeled tuple. Those twelve are now the
  explicit `?` token.

Deliberately not ported:

- The two Bug Identification tasks, which depend on live typing.
- The 27 pre-placed probe refractors these three carried (14/12/1), pre-armed
  to rich-probe-in-table mode. `Triggers.refractor_seg_to_seg` emits
  `^^probe(...)` only for a refractor whose id is a term root in the current
  parse, and these ids came from the older grammar, so 26 of the 27 dropped in
  the decode. The tasks start without them.
- The study's condition flags. `Tutorial.p` on that branch carried a
  `setting_overrides` field (`rich_probes`, `display_tables`, `read_only`)
  applied on every slide switch, plus A/B ordering from a `?treatment` URL
  parameter; none of it has a `.hzt` marker. For the record, the three tasks
  wanted: Midterm Mean `display_tables`, Overall Grade `rich_probes = false`,
  Tidy Term both.

All three start as real tasks -- a hole in Midterm Mean, an identity-stub
`tidy_term` -- so their hidden tests fail until solved. `./hazel analyze
--warnings` reports nothing on any of the three starting programs, and
tutorial-verify is 28/28.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

e2596cae91090176162b5d833c5b3355327db063Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-08T10:43:08-04:002026-09-09T12:31:31-04:00Introduce SlidePath and give tutorial lessons folders

Documentation mode already rendered "/"-separated slide names as nested
breadcrumb dropdowns, but that hierarchy was an ad-hoc convention spread
across three unrelated places. Name it.

src/web/util/SlidePath.re holds `{folders, leaf}` plus the operations both
modes need: parse/render, folder grouping, the folder-scoped index arithmetic
the navigation arrows use, and the breadcrumb decomposition.
EditorModeView.indicator_select is now a thin render over
SlidePath.breadcrumb and takes list(SlidePath.t), shedding the inline
take/nth/dedup_f work and two latent crashes with it: a Failure("nth")
whenever one slide name was a proper prefix of another, and an Option.get.
Init.re builds its "Derivations / " prefix through the type rather than a
literal separator.

Tutorial lessons then carry their folder in @title, and both arrow surfaces
(in-slide and top bar) step within the current lesson's folder, clamping at
its edges so the last lesson of a folder shows the completion message instead
of a next arrow. Crossing folders goes through the dropdown. The folders are
Basics x19 (through Labeled Tuple Projection), Tuple Structural Operations
x3, and Tables x3 -- the table lessons plus the rich probe built for tables.

Retitling lessons is safe: identity is the id=<uuid> flag, not the title, so
saved progress survives the recategorization.

Test_SlidePath covers the type and its navigation, including the
proper-prefix case that used to raise. Test_Tutorial ratchets the authored
lessons: the folder set and sizes, contiguity, that Basics / Holes stays
first as the default lesson, and that repeated next/prev traverses exactly
one folder and then stops.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

5570421327429ad638791dc3fbc542307337bcf8Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-09T12:28:54-04:002026-09-09T12:28:54-04:00Mark read-only code in lesson prose as a display, not the editor

A locked cell in a lesson's prompt looked like the editor the student types
in, down to sharing its I-beam cursor. It now reads as a framed display.

The tint moves off `.code` and onto the cell. `.code` is only as wide as its
text, so it painted each line separately and ended ragged on the right with
the cell's surface showing through the gaps; on the cell it covers the result
row too. Its colour moves from --Y0 to --T1: --Y0 is the menu fill, and at
0.05 chroma it is twice the neutral ramp, which is what made it shout. --T1
puts these cells on the ramp that already exists -- page --SAND 99%, cell
--T1 97%, result strip --T3 91%.

The hairline is a box-shadow ring rather than a border. A border insets the
children while they keep the cell's own 6.4px radius, so their fill cuts
inside the curve and leaves a sliver at the bottom corners where the result
row rounds off; a ring draws outside the padding box, so the radius is shared
and the corners agree. No radius is set here at all -- `#main.Tutorial .cell`
already owns that value, and a class-only selector could not override it.

Locked cells get `cursor: default`. `#main .code-editor` gives the primary
editor an I-beam and was catching these too, so the cursor distinguished
nothing; the two locked panels were not even consistent with each other.
Text stays selectable so examples can be copied.

Inline code keeps --Y0 and is exempted from the cell rule: marking a token
inside a sentence is a different job from marking a read-only region, and the
two can diverge. The Task Reference panel is excluded as well --
`.task-reference-section` is already a card with this same fill and border,
and its body clips at exactly the cell's width, so a ring outside the cell
box survives only along the top edge.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
