d2d23dfb5e148cecad71c0fa635e3c83aec9caf6Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-09T12:28:39-04:002026-09-09T12:28:39-04:00Pin the .hzt round-trip as a test, and say which holes survive it

Review asked whether `tutorial-verify` should be a test as well. It should:
the README asserts every lesson is a fixed point of the text round-trip, and
nothing was checking it. `Test_TextRoundtrip.re` already owns that property
and already had a per-slide DocSlides group, so the lessons go in beside them
as TutorialLessons rather than into a new suite that could not be told apart
from it -- 50 cases, each lesson's impl and hidden tests, reusing the
existing per-slide helper. Verified it fails on a dropped hole rather than
rubber-stamping: authoring `[¿]` reddens exactly `List Literals (impl)`.

The README's hole note was also answering the wrong question -- how Hazel's
text format spells holes, which belongs to MarkerParse, not to lesson
authoring. It now says what an author needs: holes are `¿`, the Grout the
student fills. `[?]` is called out as a workaround for the round-trip
dropping a lone `¿` inside a container (#2518), naming the one lesson that
needs it so it can be undone, and `?` in type position is distinguished from
both since lesson 22 uses it for the unknown type.

The `wrapper` note now answers what was asked: it binds the whole @code as
`answer`, so the tests see that and nothing else the impl defined; without
it `EditorUtil.append_exp` recurses into `Let` bodies, so the tests land
inside the impl's own let-chain and every binding it introduces is in scope.

