25f29c8991167c619be3ebec49c6d7891ee1766dAlexander Bandukwala2026-08-24T09:33:31-04:002026-09-09T11:27:47-04:00Use Core's list and option combinators where we hand-rolled them

Net -94 lines across 32 files. Follows the src/util idioms pass, now that
every library is opened over Core.

Deleted, having judged them not worth keeping:

  - ListUtil.rev_if -- a one-line combinator (b ? List.rev : Fn.id) with
    no Core equivalent, but `b ? List.rev(xs) : xs` at the call site reads
    better than a helper, and there were no call sites.
  - ListUtil.intersection_f -- the operation is reasonable but this
    implementation is O(n*m) over Poly.equal, which is a trap to leave
    lying around unused; the Core-idiomatic version would go through a
    Set. Both had tests and no production callers, so the tests went too
    (3323 -> 3320).

Kept but simplified, because Core has no equivalent for either:

  - StringUtil.trim_trailing_whitespace trims per *line*, which
    String.rstrip alone does not do. Its 22 lines of char-list reversal
    become String.rstrip(~drop=...) mapped over the split, 9 lines.
  - OptUtil.unzip distributes an option over a pair. Expressible as two
    Option.maps rather than a switch.

Replaced with Core combinators:

  - 42 List.length(x) == 0 / > 0 -> List.is_empty. Line-neutral but O(1)
    instead of O(n); several were on segment-scale lists.
  - 7 List.concat(List.map(...)) -> List.concat_map.
  - List.hd_exn(List.rev(x)) -> List.last_exn (2), List.map |> List.rev ->
    List.rev_map (1).
  - One switch in CardProj was a pure identity wrapper over its scrutinee
    and is gone entirely.
  - Three `| None => Some(z) | Some(z) => Some(z)` switches collapse to
    Some(... |> Option.value(~default=z)).
  - Three JSON field lookups in OpenRouter become Option.first_some.

On that last point: ocamlgrep finds 24 of those identity-Option-rewrap
switches, but only 7 are safe to convert. Option.first_some(a, b)
evaluates b eagerly, and most of the None branches here are recursive
calls (find_last_tile(rest), scan(tail)), monadic binds, or -- in
Measured.find_by_id -- a printf. Converting those would turn an
early-exit into unconditional work, so they stay as switches.

make test-quick green at 3320; src stanzas release-clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

8b24e808eaf943efb1a1b2fcd5d96cdcd53f3d6dAlexander Bandukwala2026-08-21T14:16:32-04:002026-09-09T11:27:47-04:00Migrate test/ to Jane Street Core

Opens Core over the haz3ltest executable -- 103 files, 49k lines. 61 files
touched, +1491/-1217. With this every dune stanza in the repo is on Core.

Test code leans on Poly harder than anything else (28 of 103 files),
which is expected: assertions compare whole terms, zippers and results,
and Poly is the equality those comparisons already used.

Two failures worth recording, both mine:

  - A name-based sweep prefixing the deprecated top-level primitives with
    Stdlib. rewrote occurrences *inside string literals*. Those strings are
    Hazel program text and expected values -- Test_Atom asserts on the
    builtin named "string_of_int", Test_TableTransforms embeds Hazel source
    -- so the sweep turned a passing test into `FAIL has
    Stdlib.string_of_int`. This is the one class of damage the type checker
    cannot see; only running the tests caught it. Reverted by re-scanning
    with a string-literal-aware pass.
  - That revert then stripped two *pre-existing* Stdlib.Buffer calls in
    Test_MenhirFuzz, because the literal scanner mis-paired a quote.
    Restored by diffing the Stdlib. count against HEAD per file.

Other shapes specific to test code: Core.Random.State.t is not
Stdlib.Random.State.t and QCheck wants the latter, so the seeded
generators use Stdlib.Random; Printexc.register_printer and the file-IO
primitives take Stdlib.; and ocamlgrep's range for a comparison that ends
a statement can include the trailing semicolon, which lands inside the
generated call.

make test-quick is green at 3323 tests. Note that test-quick skips the
QCheck property suites, so the ten files mentioning QCheck compile and
load but do not execute here -- worth a full `make test` before this
lands.

test/ does not build clean under the release profile, but it did not
before either: Test_ReparseDocSlides has an unused `open EditingPrelude`
that is present unchanged on dev. Left alone -- enforcing release warnings
on test/ is what ci-cache-and-job-split is for.

