0de55d35d55832e984784709466a5cf5f998dae2Alexander Bandukwala2026-08-21T13:59:33-04:002026-09-09T11:27:46-04:00Migrate src/haz3lcore to Jane Street Core

Open Core over the haz3lcore library -- 115 files, 37k lines, and the last
layer between language and web. 78 files touched, +2357/-1990.

This one went through a scripted toolkit built from the traps the previous
three libraries turned up, so the first build after the flip had 2 errors
rather than dozens. The remainder were shapes the earlier libraries had
not shown:

  - Token.re is a compatibility shim that re-exports stdlib functions
    point-free (let sub = String.sub, let concat = String.concat, let
    sort_uniq = List.sort_uniq(compare), ...). Point-free aliases pick up
    Core's argument order and labels silently, so each got an explicit
    arity: (s, pos, len) => String.sub(s, ~pos, ~len), and so on.
  - Operator sections. `(==)(string_delim)` is polymorphic equality
    partially applied; six of these needed a witness, and they were not
    all strings -- Skel compares a Token.t list (List.equal(String.equal))
    and Segment compares a Sort.t (Sort.equal).
  - `let compare = compare;` inside a comparator module was polymorphic
    under the stdlib and becomes int-only under Core, which breaks the
    Set.Make it feeds. Reassociate's ShardKey is (Id.t, list(int)), so it
    takes Poly.compare.
  - String.rindex_opt / index_opt lose the _opt suffix; String.fold_left
    becomes String.fold with ~init and ~f; top-level max_int is
    Int.max_value.

Equality splits as before: named witnesses where the type is concrete,
nothing for int, and Poly for structural comparison of whole terms and
zippers. 43 of 115 modules take `open Poly` -- high, like web, because
zipper and projector code compares tiles, molds and shapes constantly.

One note on verification: the release profile promotes warning 33 to an
error, so an `open Poly` that the compiler-driven pass added and later
edits made redundant fails release while dev stays green. Worth building
release explicitly after this kind of pass.

make test-quick stays at 3323 tests; release profile is warning-free.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2d59f229c8f4e17f532a0bb4b44d9669a2a973eeAlexander Bandukwala2026-08-21T11:02:58-04:002026-09-09T11:27:46-04:00Replace hand-rolled util helpers with their Core equivalents

Now that src/util is opened over Core, a number of helpers here are
reimplementing functions Core already ships. Delegate to Core and delete
the hand-rolled bodies: 142 lines out, 34 in.

Two of these were already marked as waiting on a newer stdlib, and Core
supplies both today:

  - ListUtil.take round-tripped through Stdlib.List.to_seq / Seq.take /
    of_seq under a "remove once List.take is available in ocaml 5.3"
    comment. Core has List.take. (Note Core's lazy-sequence type is
    Sequence, not Seq; nothing here needs it.)
  - StringUtil.split_first hand-rolled index-and-substring under a "drop
    this when the compiler pin reaches 5.5" comment. Core has
    String.lsplit2.

Two pairs turn out to be duplicates of each other, which only became
visible once both sides were expressed in Core terms:

  - ListUtil.join and ListUtil.intersperse had byte-identical bodies.
    Both are List.intersperse.
  - ListUtil.truncate and ListUtil.take both mean "first n". Both are
    List.take.

is_suffix_of now calls List.is_suffix, which makes its two private
helpers -- max_common_suffix and common_suffix_length -- dead, so they
are gone. Neither was exported.

combine_opt and map2_opt stop catching Invalid_argument out of the _exn
variants and match Core's Or_unequal_lengths result instead, which
expresses the length check in the type rather than through the exception
handler.

Otherwise: hd_opt/last_opt are List.hd/List.last, rev_concat is
List.rev_append, unzip3 is List.unzip3, count_pred is List.count,
findi_opt is List.findi, remove_first_n is List.drop, and in OptUtil get,
map2, some_if, zip, filter and sequence are value_or_thunk, map2,
some_if, both, filter and all.

One deliberate behavioural change: OptUtil.traverse was built on
fold_right, so it applied f right-to-left; it is now
sequence(List.map(l, ~f)), which applies f left-to-right. f is still
applied to every element in both versions, so only the order differs.

Public signatures are unchanged, so nothing outside src/util is touched.
make test-quick stays at 3323 tests, and Test_ListUtil already covers the
take/truncate/drop/slice boundaries.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

a7586479ed1effee799d4aabb13a3f0a2dc03d44Alexander Bandukwala2026-08-21T12:00:56-04:002026-09-09T11:27:46-04:00Migrate src/language to Jane Street Core

Open Core over the language library, following src/util. Like util, the
library is the atomic unit: -open Core applies to the whole dune stanza,
so all 112 modules move together. 75 files touched, +2017/-1738.

Wiring matches util's -- flags on the library stanza so :standard keeps
inheriting dune-workspace's warning config, plus core in (libraries).

The bulk is mechanical: 538 labelled arguments (~f, ~init, ~compare), 102
typed equality witnesses, 31 List.is_empty / Option.is_some in place of
comparisons against [] and None, and 28 Stdlib.* prefixes.

The interesting part is the 67 conversions to _exn variants. Several
stdlib functions that raise have total counterparts in Core under the
same name, so the flip changes their return type rather than failing to
compile:

  - List.hd / tl / nth return options in Core. IdTagged.rep_id is
    List.hd(ids); under Core it silently became Id.t option and only
    broke at its use sites in MPat and Sig. Converted to hd_exn / tl_exn
    / nth_exn to keep the raising behaviour.
  - List.find likewise. Coverage.re had the raising stdlib find; it is
    find_exn now. The nine List.find_opt call sites are the ones that
    correctly become plain List.find.
  - List.map2 / for_all2 / fold_left2 / zip return Or_unequal_lengths
    rather than raising, so they became the _exn variants. Where the
    result was already being pattern-matched for length failure
    (combine_opt, map2_opt in util) the variant is better, but here the
    callers expect the exception.

Three shadowing traps, all silent under -open Core:

  - Local Map.Make / Set.Make now resolve to Core's comparator-based
    functors, whose interfaces differ. Ctx, Coverage, RuleVerify,
    LabeledTuple and BuiltinsTupleOperations get Stdlib. prefixes.
  - Float.modf returns a Parts record, not a tuple, and Core deprecates
    the top-level float primitives. BuiltinsBase backs Hazel's own float
    builtins, so those use Stdlib.ceil, Stdlib.sin, Stdlib.modf and so
    on -- exact behaviour rather than a plausible Core equivalent.
  - Int.max_int / min_int are max_value / min_value.

Equality is split deliberately. Where the type is concrete the witness is
named: String.equal, Id.equal, Bigint.equal, Option.equal, List.equal, or
the equal function [@deriving eq] already generates (equal_cls,
equal_op_bin, equal_projector_data, InvalidOperationError.equal,
DrvSort.equal). Int comparisons need nothing, since Core permits = on
int. That leaves whole-term and whole-record structural comparison, which
is what Poly is for: 26 explicit Poly.equal / phys_equal call sites, plus
open Poly in the four modules whose job is structural comparison --
Equality, MatchExp, DrvTermBase and Operators. Typ.re was kept under
Core's discipline and its six sites fixed individually, being the file
where an accidental equality would matter most.

The equality sites were found with ocamlgrep's typed patterns, which also
kept 33 hits out of the diff: [@deriving eq] synthesises equal functions
whose code is attributed back to the type declaration, so those matches
point at 'BadToken(string)' and 'time: float' rather than at any
comparison.

Public signatures are unchanged; haz3lcore, web and the CLI needed no
edits. make test-quick stays at 3323 tests and the release profile is
warning-free.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

