cb8a0c1046c667a4966d126e1ef865b340cde286Alexander Bandukwala2026-08-21T11:27:49-04:002026-09-09T11:27:46-04:00Make equality explicit at the type in src/language

Reason's == and != elaborate to OCaml's = and <>, which are polymorphic
structural comparison. Spell out which equality is meant instead:
String.equal, Char.equal, Float.equal, Bool.equal. Behaviour is unchanged
-- these are the same comparisons, named.

This is a prerequisite for opening Core over this library, where = and <>
are restricted to int, but it stands on its own: at a call site like
Typ.re's `(Var(n1), Var(n2)) => n1 == n2` it is worth saying out loud that
two type variables are being compared by name.

The sites were found with ocamlgrep rather than grep, using typed patterns
('(__ : string) = (__ : string)'). That matters because most of them are
variable-to-variable -- name1 == name2, str == y, v == w -- with no literal
for a text search to key on. 126 comparisons across 20 files: 111 string,
5 char, 6 bool, 4 float.

Two things the typed search revealed that a text pass would have gotten
wrong:

  - 33 further hits point at type declarations, not comparisons
    ('BadToken(string)', 'time: float'). Those are the equal functions
    synthesised by [@deriving eq], attributed back to the declaration
    site. Editing them would have corrupted the types. They are filtered
    out by requiring the matched source text to actually contain == or !=.
  - int comparisons need no change at all, since Core permits = on int.
    That is 92 sites in this library left untouched.

Three of the bool sites were `== true` / `== false` against a literal;
those collapse to the predicate itself rather than gaining a Bool.equal.

make test-quick stays at 3323 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

