Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

9dd7911bdb0846613eef4f31543e378855c5e68aAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-07T17:22:36-04:002026-09-09T08:02:08-04:00Standard library: group the builtins into modules, ship SHOW and ORD instances

Now that modules are real values the standard library can be one. `List`,
`String`, `Int`, `Float`, `Option` and `Pair` are module builtins, so the 65
list functions and the string family are reachable as `List.map` and
`String.length` instead of only as top-level `map` and `string_length`.

Every builtin keeps its flat name. `String.length` and `string_length` are
the same function, so no program, slide or exercise changes meaning; the
modules are the front door from here. Both halves of a module builtin are
derived from the same builtin records, the signature's member types and the
module's member values, so a member cannot drift from the function it names,
and a member is the implementation rather than a copy of it: it is the term
the flat name is bound to.

A module builtin is an ordinary constant whose type is a signature and whose
value is a module with already-evaluated items, because the environment
hands a program values: a module with pending items would arrive unevaluated
and its projections would not reduce. A member that holds a fixpoint still
has to unroll, so the projection now judges a definition the way a variable
lookup does, sharing one predicate. That is the only evaluator change, and
only a module out of the environment can hit it.

`SHOW` and `ORD` ship as signature types with an instance per base type
(`ShowInt`, `ShowFloat`, `ShowBool`, `ShowString`, `OrdInt`, `OrdFloat`,
`OrdString`), each with a manifest type member since resolution selects on
it. They are deliberately NOT ambient: a program marks what it wants, one
line per instance. Ambient instances are a language decision rather than a
library one and need a priority or coherence rule first, because with none a
program's own instance for a base type makes every call ambiguous against
the shipped one; twelve of the implicits tests change meaning if they are
ambient, which is the same warning from the other side.

No EQ: polymorphic `==` already compares two values of a type, so an
equality signature earns its keep only once a type can choose its own
equality, which wants the sharing constraint the docs already call for.

Tests: `Statics.BuiltinsModules` (11) on the signature side, member types,
a missing member, the signature aliases and instance resolution;
`Evaluator.BuiltinsModules` (10) on the value side, including that a member
equals its flat function and that a recursive member unrolls through a
projection. `docs/modules.md` gains a section and a Key Files row.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

1f7b84c39c5a908cae93a4d0dcbaf9351e1dc3fdAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-06T22:19:27-04:002026-09-09T08:02:08-04:00Modular implicits (2/3): resolution at applications, marks, views

`implicit S : SIG` binders are implicit instances in their scope
(Ctx.ImplicitEntry, mark_implicit, implicit_instances). At an application
whose domain has implicit components (MatchedTyp.implicit_components), the
new Implicits.re decides how each is supplied: a literal tuple's items are
the components, and an implicit one takes the current item only when items
beyond the explicit ones remain and it is a module fitting the signature
(leftover items with a single explicit component make the whole tuple that
component's argument). It resolves the rest from the instances in scope, narrowed by the
explicit arguments' types and then the expected type, instantiates the
binders in the later components and the codomain (substitution by the
instance's path; avoidance when unresolved), analyzes the argument against
the instantiated explicit components and splices the instances into the
elaborated argument with ids derived from the application's, so the runtime
sees an ordinary application and incremental evaluation re-keys on it. A
tuple-typed argument that is not a literal is destructured around them.

Marks: ImplicitNotFound / ImplicitAmbiguous on the application (a hole is
passed so evaluation proceeds around the error), ImplicitBinderNotModule and
ImplicitBinderPosition on binders; Message.ImplicitResolved for the
inspector; CLI and inspector renderings. A failed resolution names the type
members the call requires of the instance, "no implicit instance of SHOW
with T = String": matching each component that mentions the binder against
the type supplied there, and the codomain against the expected type, reads
them off (Implicits.member_eqs). Both marks name the signature only when
there is one, so an unannotated binder no longer renders as a blank type.
`Fun` keeps its implicit binders nameable in the codomain and renames an
expected arrow's binders to the pattern's; the type checker scopes a
domain's binders over the later components and the codomain
(TypExpectation.ArrowDomainExpected).

Typ.ana_meet gains componentwise tuple subtyping (a module passed for an
implicit parameter arrives in a tuple) and treats an unknown arrow domain as
matching any domain.

Tests: Test_Statics_Implicits (39), Test_Evaluator_Implicits (15), an
elaboration pin of the spliced argument; has_mark_test and single_mark_test
move to the statics prelude. Three of those tests are one program written
twice: an implicit binder keeps the caller's type equation, while the same
parameter written plainly loses it to part 4's avoidance and checks the call
against nothing. The second is a pinned gap, documented as such here and in
docs/modules.md, not intended behavior.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

5d068b800ae05fb2a2f333e57c17deb98261ed4dAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-05T11:01:46-04:002026-09-09T08:02:07-04:00Error reporting: tuple labels like modules, named missing members, CLI spans

- Labeled tuples follow the module design for bad projections: `t.y` with
  no label y marks the label (`LabelNotFound`, now worded "Tuple has no
  label y; its labels are x") while the dot carries only a message
  (`Message.TupleLabelNotFound`). Tuples and lists of tuples alike.
- Any expression of signature type analyzed against a signature that
  declares members it lacks gets `ModuleMissingMembers` instead of a
  generic mismatch (`StaticsBase.expectation_mismatch_mark`), so module
  variables, function arguments and function results name what is
  missing, as module literals did. The computation lives in
  `Sig.missing_members`, shared with `ModuleHelpers`. Slide item 26a.
- `hazel analyze` carets span every measured id of the diagnosed term, so
  `m.x` or `a + b` is underlined whole rather than at its operator tile,
  as the editor highlights it. Ids without a measurement (terms inside a
  folded projector) are skipped silently.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

cf045a027edefb48471da5b37f859009dea9903fAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-05T13:14:51-04:002026-09-09T08:02:07-04:00Modules: expectations reach sub-modules and destructured binders

When a module is checked against a signature, `ModuleHelpers.modlet_pat`
annotated only a bare variable binder with its expected member type. It
now annotates every variable inside a destructuring pattern
(`let (a, b) = …` is checked as `(a : Int, b : Int)`, so a wrong component
is reported on that component) and `module Inner = …` items, which receive
the sub-signature the outer signature declares for them, so a member the
sub-module lacks, a differing type member, or a wrong definition is
reported inside the sub-module rather than as one mismatch on the outer
module. A binder the user annotated keeps its annotation. The refold strips
the synthetic ascriptions by walking the user's pattern and its elaboration
together.

Slide items 20a and 20b; tests for nested modules and tuple, labeled
tuple, nested tuple and cons destructuring.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

fd71820009929a7052ee4c0d1be49eb68705e558Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-05T15:31:47-04:002026-09-09T08:02:07-04:00Signatures: a member declared twice is an error

A signature could declare the same member more than once and silently
keep the last declaration. Now the later declaration is marked
(`Mark.SigDuplicateMember`, on the signature item itself: `Info.sig_`
gains `marks`, read by the cursor inspector, the problem sidebar and the
CLI). Value and module members share a namespace; type members, manifest
or abstract, have their own, so `let x` and `type x` may coexist. Module
bodies are unchanged: there a repeated binding is shadowing.

Slide item 23a; tests for each member kind and for the mark landing on the
later item.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

9a3ee79c82f7b50a6a84e40700134a856c0f94c4Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-06T18:07:41-04:002026-09-09T08:02:07-04:00Modular implicits (1/3): `implicit S : SIG` binders, syntax and type layer

New pattern and type forms `Pat.Implicit(mpat)` / `Typ.Implicit(mpat)`: an
implicit module binder as a component of a function parameter and of an
arrow domain, in scope for the later components and the codomain. Type
operations (free_vars, path_roots, subst, avoid, normalize, meet, ana_meet,
equal_up_to_aliases, pretty_print) thread the binder; `subst_path_root`
renames or instantiates it. `Pat.of_mpat` replaces the module helper's
converter; `MPat` gains binder accessors.

Editor: Pat tile `implicit <MPat>` at a precedence between `:` and `::`;
Typ tile `implicit <MPat> : <Typ>` (two shards, since the type-level comma
binds tighter than the MPat's `:`). Menhir productions, converter both ways,
printer, QCheck generators and shrinkers.

Also fixes two pre-existing inconsistencies the new syntax exposed: the
type-level dot was looser than the type arrow in the editor (`A -> M.T` read
as `(A -> M).T`; now `Precedence.type_dot`), and the pattern-annotation
parenthesization was not idempotent on reparse (`paren_typ_always`).

Statics only binds the name for now; resolution follows in 2/3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

37c76e993e8ea15c981950cd0fd653291953af8dAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-09T08:01:59-04:002026-09-09T08:01:59-04:00Stop re-parenthesizing the content of a Parens type

Printing a Sig was not idempotent: `type T = { ... }` came out as
`type T = ({ ... })`, and each further trip added another layer, so
`(({ ... }))`, `((({ ... })))`. That is why the Menhir fuzz property's
counterexamples could not be pinned with the round-trip helpers.

parenthesize_typ's Parens case ran its content through
paren_typ_at(Precedence.min). Precedences count down from max = 0, so that
test wraps exactly the types whose precedence IS min -- a Sig, a bare sum, a
multihole -- and it wrapped them again on every pass. The wrapper being
emitted is already the protection, so the content needs none: nothing outside
can bind into it. already_paren, which the Prod cases below already consult
for the same reason, is still passed down.

Extends the sig-member test to reprint its own output, which pins the layer
count rather than just the spacing.

