47ade7dd643b1138a39b0e9d56a1f5aa8ae6d3c4Alexander Bandukwala2026-09-06T09:14:38-04:002026-09-08T16:22:53-04:00Functions: report a body mismatch once

A function analyzed against an arrow type whose body mismatched the
expected return type was marked twice: on the body, and again on the
function, whose synthesized type was built from the already-marked body.
As for a tuple component, a body that reports its mismatch contributes the
expected type to the function's type, so the function is not reported
again. Fixes #2507.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2e7a7af6e222db53cd6f622c1245b1e860d1b11dAlexander Bandukwala2026-09-05T13:31:55-04:002026-09-08T16:22:53-04:00Tuples: report a component mismatch once, describe shape mismatches

Fixes #1958.

A tuple literal checked against a tuple type reported a wrong component
three times: on the component, on the tuple, and on the parentheses
around it, each with the two full types. Now a component that already
reported a mismatch against its expected type contributes that type to the
tuple's synthesized type, so the tuple (and its parentheses) no longer
repeat it; a labeled element `l=e` does the same for its value. Only the
component is marked.

The tuple still reports mismatches of its own shape, and now says what
they are instead of printing both types: a different arity ("Expecting a
tuple of 2 elements but got 3 elements") or different labels ("... with
labels name, age but got 1 element with labels name; missing label age").
`LabeledTupleStaticsHelpers.shape_mismatch` computes the summary for both
the CLI and the cursor inspector. A same-shape mismatch on a non-literal
(a variable of tuple type) keeps the generic message, where the two types
are the useful information.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

