b1d83917ce5959399f1066c9ecb8fc5888dce64adisconcision2026-09-07T02:10:17-07:002026-09-07T02:10:17-07:00Constellation mode + the info panel under the canvas

The info panel (where the focus strip was, canvas column only) gains
side tabs — values ≡ · definition ƒ · tests ✓ — in every placement.
Content-sized up to 240 px; the handle on its top edge drags an
explicit height (persisted), double-click returns to content-sized.
The legend moves into the values tab's empty state.

Constellation mode (Settings.canvas_main, header button ▣ mode /
⇱ editor): the canvas fills the main area and the editor stack renders
inside the definition tab, one definition at a time. Every pick is
FocusDef: outline plain/cmd click, canvas edge / alias / module click,
go-to-definition inside the cell (cross_cell_target ~single), test
rows. One selection, shared indication: the stack's single entry
highlights its outline row and its canvas edge/node and feeds the
values tab; a glyph node click only overrides values/halo. The view
you click in holds still, the other follows: canvas → outline row
scrolls into view; outline → CanvasCamera.reveal pans minimally.

Tests tab: tests MENTIONING the selection (CanvasGraph.exp_refs
resolves qualified paths like Pos.manhattan — a module name parses as
a constructor; subject attribution uses it too, so member edges now
carry test pips), subject tests marked, row click opens the test.

Height vs camera: the layout is framed as if the panel were closed
(layout_height), the camera sees the visible part (avail_height).
Outside the mode the definition tab is a read-only preview with an
↗ editor jump (CanvasDefPreview, memoized on the buffer). FocusDef on
the already-single entry is a no-op (keeps the live cell); FocusDef
moves the selection to the cell. Sidebar's Canvas tab shows a note
while the mode is on. Harness: __canvasTests(), __programText().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

c64fd3641f202295cd20c7021719c0241847db63disconcision2026-09-07T02:10:17-07:002026-09-07T02:10:17-07:00Perf journal: name actions by constructor, not by their sexp

The action census (066fe5a1d4) serialized EVERY action to sexp to take
its head; evaluation-result actions carry values whose closure
environments serialize exponentially, so the first edit inside a
stacked cell froze the page for minutes (three of three tries; the
hang stack was sexp_of_exp_t all the way down). The slow-update /
slow-calc / edit-action names had the same latent problem. A
hand-written constructor matcher replaces all four; Log.Entry.save
warns when a log entry takes over 50 ms to serialize.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

066fe5a1d48c4152233747aa603ac2d9013d9746disconcision2026-09-06T23:42:48-07:002026-09-06T23:42:48-07:00Streamed chat tokens no longer re-render the editor; canvas load trims

The multi-second stutters during a live run: every streamed LLM delta
re-rendered the whole app, and on a 7k-char program each render cost
50-105 ms (the code editor rebuilt in full: the CachedSyntax record was
reallocated by the cursor-only calculate branch so the code-text memo
never hit, the completion engine ran unmemoized, the id watchdog walked
the buffer, and a hover-rule explainer parsed two editors per render).
At token rate that saturates the main thread. Measured with a 20/s
burst of 60 ticks (__streamBurst): 53 tasks over 50 ms before, none
after.

- CachedSyntax.calculate keeps the record's identity when only
  selection ids were recomputed and are unchanged
- QuiverDec memoizes the engine result on the engine segment; the
  segment itself is memoized on the cached display segment + selection
- IdWatch skips physically-unchanged buffers
- HoverRuleSpec memoizes on rule/font metrics/settings
- HighLevelNodeMap.build_for memoizes on the items record (the canvas
  asked twice per render)

Sustained load during a score (run 4, 50-60 s window, main thread
busy 82% -> ~60%):
- CanvasRipple: coarser cells (7 -> 9), double-buffered field, half-
  rate stepping, sponge over the band only with int compares, dirty-
  region redraw (only the live region of the lattice is cleared and
  redrawn while the viewport is still), peak-based liveness
- CanvasBubble placement loop idles while hidden and writes only on
  change
- Main: an action census in the perf journal (action/<kind>)
- CanvasTrajectory: __streamBurst(n, ms) probe; CanvasRipple:
  __rippleCounters()

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

03f4c2badc86eff7f930473b551cfcd4f75324b8disconcision2026-09-06T23:42:48-07:002026-09-06T23:42:48-07:00Canvas: module hulls glide on the nodes' schedule

On a scored render the jelly circles used to spring toward their new
targets from the moment of the render, while the nodes waited for the
drift act and then moved over relayout_ms — the metaballs ran ahead of
their nodes. Now a scored render sets the hull targets with a glide
(same start, same duration, ease-out) and only unscored renders (drags,
collapses) spring.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

6fd3d43a231be8ab223404973a419ce3ca82ef98disconcision2026-09-06T21:16:56-07:002026-09-06T21:16:56-07:00canvas: the layout keeps its natural spacing; the camera does the fitting

andrew: everything is put in the same place. The layout was squeezed to
0.7× to fill the pane and the camera then zoomed out on top of it.
- pane-fit x-scale floor 0.7 -> 1.0 (never tighter than natural size)
- column/row gaps 126/72 -> 160/96
- the camera shows the whole program down to zoom 0.5 (was 0.7), the
  same floor the score's framing uses; below that it follows the actor
- product formation runs on the beat's tempo (it ignored compression, so
  a 40-act module insert could not shorten)

545c25cb9628cf850d87107cd439909022904ef8disconcision2026-09-06T20:34:20-07:002026-09-06T20:34:20-07:00canvas: fold local aliases before reading the arrow; frame a beat whole, then hold

Watching andrew's dungeon run 3 at 1x (contact sheets of the 26-act
Dungeon insert):

- `width : Room -> Int` arrived inlined as `(Int, Int, [Tile]) -> Int`
  and the canvas read it as a three-argument function with a product of
  glyphs far below the module. The local-alias fold now runs bottom-up
  over the member's WHOLE type before the arrow is flattened, so `[Tile]`
  is recognized inside `(Int, Int, [Tile])` and the domain is `Room`.
- Framing (C3): a beat's targets are framed whole before its first act
  and held; per-act frames only when an act's targets leave the view.
  Unseen nodes (C2) are a SOFT set: included when they fit, dropped when
  they cannot — a required set the view could not hold at the minimum
  zoom made the minimal pan chase one edge then the other every act
  (100 px rocking); without the soft set the camera panned 200–500 px per
  act following the actor. Now: one zoom-out and one pan for the whole
  module. A target already in view (even inside the margin band) holds.
- A label leader that appears on an EXISTING edge after the relayout is
  owned by the drift (it journaled as an ORPHAN).

Dungeon run 3, refactor smoke, nested growth: 0 orphans. Test_SnapProbe
covers the nested fold (Room = (Int, Int, [Tile])).

8fa78c5d475089e6796e2d9fde28b2378347073bdisconcision2026-09-06T20:10:16-07:002026-09-06T20:10:16-07:00canvas: member functions attach to the type they name; final ctx from the last binding

Watching the nested-growth trajectory (a module growing one member at a
time, plans/agent-canvas-trajectories/nested-growth.json):

- `add : (P, P) -> P` was drawn from a product of `(Int, Int)` glyphs
  to a separate `(Int, Int)` glyph far from `P` and outside the module's
  hull, then the layout jumped. Statics inlines a module's LOCAL aliases in
  its exported member types on purpose (P is unbound outside the braces);
  CanvasGraph now folds them back: a component that prints like a local
  alias's body — the nearest enclosing module's first — attaches to that
  alias node (refold_local).
- Under per-item statics, members arrived as VALUE badges and only became
  edges at the last step: the canvas read the program's type environment
  from the trailing expression's info, and that item is a clean reuse whose
  ctx predates the new member (an item re-analyzes only when a name it
  uses changed; a hole uses none). DefStatics.final_ctx = the last
  binding's ctx_out; the canvas reads that when the record has items.

Test_SnapProbe: the incremental member insert yields an edge; the edge's
endpoints are the alias.

a5b6dbf4c629ab803a7e92f21ab49c68b6ce6c22disconcision2026-09-06T19:46:56-07:002026-09-06T19:46:56-07:00canvas: the camera holds while the program is in view; bubbles name things

Watching the refactor trajectory at 1x (contact sheet, 1.5 s frames):

- The score's whole-program box included the act's targets (label
  points, arrow ends) with a 30 px margin, the camera's own box is the
  nodes with 12 px: they disagreed by 0.14 in zoom, and every scored
  beat re-zoomed (0.89 -> 0.75 -> 0.89) — and at 0.74 the rule fell to a
  minimal pan that pushed `Kind` half off the left edge for the rest of
  the run. Now: the box is the nodes, fit as tight as the camera fits it;
  the score HOLDS whenever the program and the act's targets are in view,
  never zooms in mid-score, and when the viewer had the whole program in
  view it zooms out to keep it rather than pan a node out (C1 over C4).
  One camera move for the whole run.
- The speech bubble printed internal keys ("Int@(Monster, Int)c1",
  "{}@Pos", "1 nodes"); it now says the node's label, the module's
  name, and nothing for a tidy.
- Journal: change/erase acts read "edit@damage", not "edit@·".

a3f5405aa205b566ff47c9174140ce53109ebf46disconcision2026-09-06T19:25:39-07:002026-09-06T19:25:39-07:00canvas: A2 without exception — every change on the board is the actor's act

Principle A2 (plans/agent-canvas-principles.md): nothing appears, changes,
or disappears unless the avatar has arrived there. Andrew's run 3 showed
its escape hatches at work: 6 functions revealed in one 'rest' act at a
centroid with the actor standing still ("functions appearing without the
agent doing anything").

Score (CanvasScore):
- no batch act, no rest act: every definition and every edge is its own
  act; a long beat COMPRESSES the tempo toward a floor (budget 24 s,
  floor 0.6) instead of merging acts.
- acts follow the program's order (new_node.order): the module first,
  its types, then its functions; a glyph nobody names rides the first
  function that needs it — the story used to open on '(Int, Int)' at the
  far left.
- removals are acts: an arrow is erased at its label, then its node
  vanishes (removed_edges in the diff; erase_act/removal_act).
- changes are acts: an element whose look changed (a hole filled, a
  constructor added, an error fixed) is touched by the actor and swaps
  at the cue (diff.changed, change_act, change_times).
- coverage(diff, score): ORPHAN lines for anything the score leaves
  unstaged; appear_times covers products formed by an edge act.

Sidebar / player:
- removed nodes and arrows stay on the board as ghosts from the previous
  layout until their act (leaving set); the player shrinks the node and
  retracts the arrow at the cue.
- changed elements render with their OLD record until their cue (staged
  set); the player pulses them and asks for the render that swaps.
- value badges join the diff as terminals of their type.
- Animation journals ORPHAN for an arrival the score did not schedule.

Dungeon run 3 replay: 0 orphans (was 11); refactor smoke trajectory
(update/rename/delete/extract): 0 orphans, every tool scored.
Test_CanvasScore: 9 tests (no batch, every edge ridden, coverage,
removals, changes).

e02f77eb2cebbe2d942deb3692f93bd069ece2d2disconcision2026-09-06T16:42:46-07:002026-09-06T16:42:46-07:00agent: a structural tool's editor record drops the pre-edit statics

The tool's canvas snapshot computes statics only when the record has
none; after a structural tool the record kept the OLD program's statics,
so the snapshot was of the previous program: the tool's beat showed
nothing and the new content landed later as an anonymous state change —
whole modules (hulls, members, edges) at once, with no tool attending.
Before the flip the tool path's offered record bridged this; the offer
mechanism is gone, so the record now carries no statics until the
editor's next calculate (the snapshot's own pass is a DefStatics memo
hit, ~50 ms). Dungeon run 3 replay: the insert_after beat carries its
module (weight 39) under its label again.

a17bdd7332bde0b0cb85503134775305cfc2f0d3disconcision2026-09-06T16:24:58-07:002026-09-06T16:24:58-07:00canvas: node maps from the spine (view crash on the first agent edit)

The flip missed the canvas's two node-map builds: they still ran the
monolithic walker on the editor's now per-item statics. With the caret
inside an inserted module the walk descended into the module item's
surrogate definition, whose scaffolding ids the per-item map strips —
Not_found during view, on the first agent action of a live run. (The
replay never hit it: its caret sits at the program end, where the walk
finds nothing to descend into.)

- CanvasSidebar: both builds go through HighLevelNodeMap.build_for.
- HighLevelNodeMap.build (the monolithic walker) returns None instead of
  raising on a per-item map.
- Test_SnapProbe: caret inside a module member / a let body, node map
  from the compositional record (fails with Not_found before the fix);
  plus the text-snapshot decoder and the agent-context view on the
  dungeon programs.
- The crash screen's buttons did nothing because the font-specimen
  resize observer asserted on every frame once the page was replaced;
  the observer now tolerates a vanished element. The view exception is
  also echoed to the console.

33ad6568736a248399565c07c18f9ab6a56974f9disconcision2026-09-06T13:00:15-07:002026-09-06T13:00:15-07:00agent: tool-result snapshots and diffs persisted as text (autosave 1.8 s → 7 ms)

The lag that grew over a long agent run was the autosave, not statics or
the canvas: every tool result kept the program before/after as Segment.t
(ids, molds, shapes per piece), and an insert's diff kept the WHOLE
program on both sides the same way — 17 MB of sexp for a 28-tool run,
re-serialized on the main thread one second after every edit (1.8 s per
tick by the end; the longest task in the run). The same programs as text
are 13 KB.

- AgentToolResult.diff: old_text/new_text; tool_result.before_text/
  after_text — the printed program text (the lossless slide format),
  re-parsed only when displayed or loaded back (segment_of_text).
- Main.re times the app's update/calculate/view phases and names any
  update or calculate over 100 ms by its action in the perf journal;
  ScratchPersist times each save step.

Dungeon replay, longest main-thread task: 1.97 s → 0.47 s; agent save
per tick at the end of the run: 1842 ms → 7 ms. Persisted conversations
in the old shape load as an empty agent (the load path already fails
soft); the program itself is stored separately and is unaffected.

7681cfd6099b4fef62c18918330cb32745393da0disconcision2026-09-06T03:58:20-07:002026-09-06T03:58:20-07:00editor on per-item statics; canvas and agent read the spine; reuse ring gone

The whole-program editor runs compositional statics again
(ScratchMode ~compositional=true): after an edit only the changed item
re-analyzes. What the merge had to turn off is now converged:

- CachedStatics.t carries `items` (the DefStatics analysis it was made
  from). HighLevelNodeMap.build_for reads the spine when present; the
  canvas takes error ownership from the items' own error ids instead of
  walking Info.ancestors (which per-item maps record per item).
- The tool path is items-only: Perform → CompositionGo.Public.go_items;
  the diff, the program-boundary insert, the snapshot statics and every
  display-side node map use per-item statics. items_for parses with
  MakeTerm.Incr.term_of_root like the editor, so both sides hit the same
  DefStatics memo slot, and is memoized per zipper (one analysis per
  structural action instead of two).
- Deleted: the statics reuse ring / offers / fingerprint (CachedStatics),
  the monolithic tool init and `use_items` switch (CompositionGo.Public),
  CodeWithStatics' offered-record path, mk_diff's old-statics parameter.
- Canvas parity tests (monolithic vs compositional record) on the dungeon
  program before and after the 28-tool run; the canvas journals the
  program's static error count so the replay harness can assert on it.

e031c1d28f97f0f17607804676f31814ef9e67e4disconcision2026-09-05T22:43:03-07:002026-09-05T22:43:03-07:00agent: structural tools on per-item statics (items mode), parity-fixed

The tool path (Perform Structural → CompositionGo.go_items) now takes its
statics and node map from DefStatics instead of a whole-program pass:
items-statics 40–180 ms per tool on the dungeon program vs a 0.4 s
monolithic pass. Parity with the monolithic path:

- build_from_items re-spines each item chain: a memoized item's d_node
  keeps the body it had when last analyzed (only pat+def are current), so
  root k's body := root k+1 (members end in the exports tail), giving the
  tools the same body/def target ids monolithic statics would.
- ItemsSpine (new): a view on DefStatics.merged for the tools — appends
  spine ancestors to every item's infos (node_of_cursor after an edit,
  the rename capture check both reason through ancestors) and stamps the
  module literal's info on its `;` ids (MakeTerm folds member separators
  into the literal's ids; the items view only had the rep id).
- empty program → no node map (Cant_derive), like the monolithic build.
- parity tests compare name paths (monolithic statics gives expression
  members a fresh wrapper id per run); the dungeon program as evolved by
  the 28-tool replay is tested fresh and incrementally (calc ~prev).

Two dungeon-run bugs, independent of items mode:
- is_module_member read the info's cls, which is a plain let for a
  module's LAST member (per-item: every member), so insert_after the last
  member took the expression path and added no `;` — every later member
  was swallowed into it (5 of the live run's 7 tool errors). Structural
  predicate now; repro tests in Test_MergeProbe.
- CanvasGraph e_name (vdom key + dom id) assumed unique; same-named
  siblings crashed the canvas vdom. Now `#k`-suffixed like node-map paths.

Dungeon replay: 28/28 tools, no crash, 127 nodes.

a60b1ddb5c9b444cb69e08b8a236e366a4a3e162Matthew Hammer2026-09-05T22:05:10-06:002026-09-05T22:07:10-06:00Do not let one stalled source hold up the runtime

A source that fails is fine: the loop moves on. A source that hangs is not.
Nothing in fetch or import times out on its own, so a source that stalls at
the TCP or TLS level -- rather than refusing -- parks the loop forever. No
later source is tried and no event is dispatched, which is the same stuck
"still loading" the event was added to end, reached by a different road.
That was a narrow risk with one published source; with three, and the first
of them mid-DNS-propagation, it is worth closing.

The budget is generous, because a slow connection is not a broken one and
the wasm is several megabytes. An overrunning source is set aside rather
than cancelled: if it lands later and nothing else has won by then, it is
still the runtime we wanted, so it is taken and announced.

The `abandoned` flag is load-bearing, and testing found out why. The late
claim is attached before the one inside withTimeout, so it runs first, while
wasm is still null -- without the flag it also fires on the ordinary path,
claiming a perfectly timely load as a late one and dispatching a second
event for it. Conditioning it on the search having actually moved on is what
keeps the late path to the case it is for.

Verified against a server that accepts connections and never answers: with
it first in the list, the loader waits out its budget, falls through to the
next source, and the card resolves -- one claim logged, none of them late.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

80af9a90fab6acc30fb2aa4a0d449b1920759758disconcision2026-09-05T21:01:22-07:002026-09-05T21:01:22-07:00agent: the node map built from the per-item statics engine (parity-gated)

HighLevelNodeMap.build_from_items derives the agent's addressing tree from
DefStatics' item chain: each item root is walked by its DEFINITION on the
item's own map (the item's map does not describe its hollow body — the chain
supplies the siblings that the body continuation used to), module bindings
analyzed member-granularly get their node and then their members as
children, each from the member's map; non-binding roots walk the children
their map knows. Parity with the monolithic build on the dungeon program:
same node ids, same paths (test group "Node map (dungeon program)").

Not wired into the tool path yet — plans/agent-items-convergence.md step 2.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

15360a428ab73d65a13daaa7a516eb539976fe82Matthew Hammer2026-09-05T21:56:28-06:002026-09-05T21:56:28-06:00Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/fumola-livelit-mvp' into fumola-livelit-mvp

