2e6e2feea8c566e19dcca105fc8b3fdecf376889Matthew Hammer2026-09-04T22:28:30-06:002026-09-04T22:28:30-06:00Flatten projectors on the path probes actually use

The previous commit fixed the wrong function. It flattened projectors in
CodeViewable.view_segment and claimed that was the probe path; it is not.
Projectors get their embedded-syntax view from ProjectorView.flex_code,
which routes to simple_code -- and simple_code carries the same stated
assumption, "Assume this doesn't contain projectors". So a probe on a
Fumola reference still drew a sample-count badge and no value.

Flatten in flex_code, which every projector's embedded syntax goes through,
including probe samples and their environment rows. The helper moves to
Printer, beside the projector-stripping that was already there, so the two
views that need it share one definition rather than each keeping a copy.

The syntax now keeps its parentheses. Unwrapped, a multi-part value reads
as several: "peek(`t)! = 1, true" sitting in a comma-separated environment
row or sample looks like two elements rather than one.

Verified in the running app rather than by inference: with a probe on the
card's final tuple, the environment goes from three blank rows to none,
"myPointer" reads as its reference and value, and the sample shows both
peeks inline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

