05be2f02761e1f9561f489372ba7b7157f87c321Matthew Hammer2026-09-04T21:23:54-06:002026-09-04T21:23:54-06:00Card: forcing a thunk, and repair a comment that ate its own clause

The card showed what each kind of Fumola value becomes in Hazel, but not
how to reach back for one a thunk already computed. force is what closes
that gap: a thunk is not a value, and it is values that have a Hazel
translation, so asking for the thunk itself brings across nothing. force(p)
runs the thunk p points at -- or reuses what it already computed -- and
answers with its value, which does translate.

Four editor livelits force thunks defined earlier in the card, one per
shape: Int, a pair, a variant, a record. They are added to the final tuple
as well, since a value nobody displays teaches nothing. Verified live: they
come back as 3, (1, true), Circle(3), (x = 1, y = true).

Also repairs the variant comment, which was missing its opening clause --
a "#" in the draft closed the comment early and ate it, leaving a sentence
that began mid-thought.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

3f62213e77631a159f9f45bb6d90e7507c186befMatthew Hammer2026-09-04T21:23:53-06:002026-09-04T21:23:53-06:00Keep references visible where no projector layer is drawn

Rendering a Fumola reference as a projector made it vanish from probe rows:
the probe's environment view goes through CodeViewable.view_segment, which
says outright that it assumes no projectors, and a projector piece there is
drawn as blank space -- the room reserved for a widget nobody paints. The
three rows that went missing were exactly the three pointer-valued ones.

So make that assumption true rather than merely stated: replace each
projector with the syntax it wraps before measuring. The reference then
reads as text wherever no projector layer exists, which is what it did
before it became a widget.

The test guards the property that actually broke -- that no projector piece
survives into a view that cannot draw one.

