68c40812f671b01742335a76283ddd23cfeda63dMatthew Hammer2026-09-04T21:05:19-06:002026-09-04T21:05:19-06:00Render a Fumola reference as a widget, at rendering time

A Fumola pointer translates to a FumolaPeek carrying both the program that
reads the cell and the value it holds. Both halves are worth showing, so it
should draw as a widget rather than as tokens.

The obvious way -- wrap the value in a Projector -- cannot work: the
evaluator strips a Projector, stepping to its body (Transition.re, kind
RemoveParens). These values are only ever seen in a result, on the far side
of evaluation, so a wrapper is gone by the time anyone could see it. That is
why the first attempt at this rendered as plain text.

So the projector is emitted in ExpToSegment instead, when the value is
rendered. Nothing carries it through evaluation, so there is nothing to
strip. The syntax underneath the projector is the old text form, so
unprojecting still reads.

The model (the reference and the value, both as text) lives in
Language.FumolaPeekModel, shared with the translation that fills it in:
values are built in language, which cannot look up into haz3lcore. The
projector draws from that model alone and consults neither statics nor the
syntax it wraps -- which is what lets it work in a result, rendered from a
bare segment with no info map behind it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

76a6cabd09941b449aa2e61a450078b2d939f2d3Matthew Hammer2026-09-04T20:47:44-06:002026-09-04T20:47:44-06:00Fix two bugs in the Symbol type, both found by the card

Registering a recursive type in the prelude needs the same Rec wrapper that
statics puts around a user-written recursive alias. `type X = ... X ...`
becomes Rec(X, ...) precisely so unfolding terminates; the bare sum made
Typ.normalize unfold Var("Symbol") forever, and annotating anything Symbol
failed with "normalize exceeded 1000 recursive calls".

With that fixed the card loaded but gave Name("hello") where a String was
annotated: structured symbols won everywhere. The check for "is a Symbol
expected here" went through resolve_ctr, which falls back to a context
lookup -- and since Symbol is in the prelude its constructors are always in
scope, so the answer was always yes. It now inspects the expected type
structurally and consults nothing else, so the text form comes back wherever
a Symbol is not what was asked for.

Neither was caught by the tests, which pass a hand-made type_tools that never
normalizes and has no ambient context, so they exercised neither the
recursion nor the scope fallback. The card found both on first load, which is
an argument for it being a test as much as documentation.

Verified in the editor: the card now evaluates, with symbolAsText giving
"hello" and symbolAsData giving Name("hello") from the same program.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

4f915cee875da3461fb952cb8e52f527bfe3b165disconcision2026-09-04T19:29:58-07:002026-09-04T19:29:58-07:00agent: module tooling fixes and the agent-side improvements from agent-canvas

