abc9520da1951940080a32767ddfc90ffaf587c7Matthew Hammer2026-09-04T20:02:14-06:002026-09-04T20:02:14-06:00Add a Fumola documentation card

The Fumola livelits had outgrown their entry in the builtins card, so they
get their own: a tour of Fumola syntax and of what each kind of value becomes
on the Hazel side.

It is a series of named fumola_thunk livelits, each annotated, so the
annotation states what the translation produces and Hazel checks the claim.
They cover Int, Bool, String from a symbol, unit, tuples, records, variants,
options both ways, and nesting; then cells, peek, and pointers.

Two of these carry the load. The String case is the only way to produce one
from a livelit at all, since a Hazel string literal cannot contain a quote
and so neither can the program inside one. The variant case is where the
annotation stops being decoration: a Fumola tag names no type of its own, so
the expected type is what decides which constructor is meant, which is the
concrete reason these livelits need checking mode.

Pointers get their own section. A pointer is not read as a number or a
handle: it arrives carrying the value it points at, so it has the type of
what it points to, and myTuplePointer makes that concrete by pointing at a
tuple.

Every program here was run against the runtime before being written down.

Two Hazel syntax traps, both caught by the reparse test rather than by me: a
`#` inside a comment closes it, and a trailing comma before a closing paren
does not parse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

