c756331545692f9df5491d99fd942e243279166fMatthew Hammer2026-09-04T17:56:39-06:002026-09-04T17:56:39-06:00Put the worked example in the livelits documentation slide

Three livelits over one runtime, which is the thing worth showing:

    let x  : Int = ^fumola_thunk(0, "`t", "`x := 137 + 137; 137") in
    let x_ : Int = ^fumola_editor(0, "pointer(`x)") in
    let y  : Int = ^fumola_editor(0, "force(pointer(`t))") in
    (x, x_, y)                    ->  (137, peek(`x)! = 274, 137)

The thunk writes a cell and returns 137. The first editor asks for a pointer
to that cell, which comes back as a peek value showing both the reference and
what it holds, and typing as Int, so the ascription is satisfied and
evaluation continues through it. The second editor forces the thunk the first
livelit made, by its name `t -- which is why naming a thunk explicitly
matters: it can be referred to from somewhere else.

The slide ends without a trailing hole marker. A `¿` after a complete
expression does not parse, which broke the reparse test until I dropped it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

