761feb3e6479f8113e585ec875d2a06a213b739aMatthew Hammer2026-09-04T11:58:04-06:002026-09-04T11:58:04-06:00Focus a livelit's own control, not its wrapper

Clicking a livelit that contains a text field could not be typed into: the
click focused the field, then Hazel's Focus action called focus_pointer,
which focuses the element carrying the projector id -- a wrapper div with no
tabindex. Focusing an unfocusable element drops focus to <body>, so the
widget stopped receiving keys the moment it was clicked, and the only way to
edit its model was to unproject the livelit and edit the underlying syntax.

focus_pointer now prefers a control inside the projector where there is one.
TextAreaProj does not have this problem because it puts the projector id
directly on its textarea.

This affects the js livelit too, which also renders a text input.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

f61c3aebf3760125955db6afa30e3cc4e5453c88Matthew Hammer2026-09-04T11:50:59-06:002026-09-04T11:50:59-06:00Report which runtime build a page loaded

The local and published runtimes behave identically, so without this there is
no way to tell which one a page is actually running -- which matters when the
point of a test is to exercise the published one. window.fumola.source()
answers "local" or "published", and the choice is logged on load.

