a7b5559cbbb5ba186e6ef812e003a0723d9cb815Matthew Hammer2026-09-03T20:18:39-06:002026-09-03T20:18:39-06:00Add a settings axis and separate fairness from extrapolation

A colleague raised that benchmarking headless Hazel may mislead, since much
of what makes Hazel slow is the information statics and evaluation record
for the UI. Two distinct questions were tangled in that, and they need
different answers.

Fairness between backends was already satisfied and is now stated: one
source tree, one settings record, one switch, one compiler version, only the
backend flag differs, and matching result checksums confirm both sides
compute the same answer.

Extrapolation to app-level speedup is not satisfied and cannot be by this
harness. To probe the gap rather than assume it away, evaluation now runs
under both probe_all=false and probe_all=true -- probe_all is off even in
CoreSettings.on, so the previous numbers measured a leaner path than the
editor runs. The two profiles are indistinguishable on these workloads.

Statics is also sampled, but only cold: Statics.mk is memoized, so a loop
measures cache hits. The single-shot sample is dominated by JIT warm-up (the
same program timed 16.6 ms and 3.2 ms in different profiles) and is labelled
as unusable for conclusions.

The workloads are compute-heavy programs that are textually tiny, the
opposite shape from the editor. That is why evaluation dominates statics
here, and why the result is 'the evaluator is ~3.5x faster', not 'Hazel is'.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

481ae0e084dbafe351397a5268d93ba67549e5ebMatthew Hammer2026-09-03T20:05:13-06:002026-09-03T20:05:13-06:00Fixed-precision workloads: wasm_of_ocaml is ~4x faster

Leaning on Hazel's fixed-precision types sidesteps the bignum wall. SInt
(machine int) and Float avoid Bigint entirely, so bench/fixed-*.hz uses only
those, and BigIntWasmStub swaps bignum out of the build.

Getting bignum out cascaded further than expected. Statics.re had exactly one
use of Jane Street Core (Core.Memo.general); replacing it with Util.Memo
removed Core from the evaluator's cone entirely. Sets, AST, Parser.mly and
Lexer.mll reached the bignum package directly and now go through util's
BigInt wrapper, which is portable across both builds.

The stub keeps construction and comparison total, because Builtins.ctx_init
needs them to boot, and makes arithmetic raise: a workload reaching Int or
Nat fails loudly rather than silently benchmarking 63-bit math.

Results, both backends from js_of_ocaml 6.2.0 on node 22, 20 iterations,
with matching result checksums across backends:

  sint-fib        1050 ms -> 285 ms   3.7x
  float-numeric    290 ms ->  74 ms   3.9x
  sint-list        168 ms ->  42 ms   4.0x

Between-workload differences are inside the noise -- the ordering flipped
across two runs -- so only the ~4x headline is claimed.

Also corrects a note in passing: the evaluator is explicitly trampolined
(Trampoline.re), and both tail and non-tail Hazel recursion reach depth
20000+ under js_of_ocaml. Deep recursion is not a backend risk.

Verified: dune build src bench --profile release clean, @test-quick 3457
tests passing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

90cb1eb144d875b636b7a321d16c151b1e502f13disconcision2026-09-03T19:00:45-07:002026-09-03T19:00:45-07:00canvas: enact new function edges as the avatar drawing the arrow

CanvasEnact derives a gesture from a paced beat's graph diff: for each
new function edge the avatar travels from the domain to the codomain
with the stroke revealing under it (arrowhead hidden until arrival),
then steps back to the pill as it fades in. Diff-driven, so it is a
projection of the structural edit whatever tool produced it. Runs one
frame after the beat renders via Web Animations and ends at identity,
so it never fights the vdom; the avatar's FLIP hop is cancelled in
favor of the ride. Tester: __canvasEnact("name").

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

b75325e6b7d5492b491813d1039a52ce8adafd4ddisconcision2026-09-03T18:56:59-07:002026-09-03T18:56:59-07:00canvas: fold alias-body formers into their nodes; damped layout ordering

A type alias whose body is a tuple, list or sum IS that former (one
object with a name), so it is drawn once: components feed the alias
node directly and the node wears the former's glyph as a badge, with
the name below (as module nodes already do). Anonymous formers written
inline in signatures keep their own product nodes. The authoring tools
no longer pin a separate former.

GraphLayout: within-rank ordering is a damped adjacent exchange — a
node passes a neighbor only when its barycenter wins by more than
order_hysteresis (0.6 rows). Ties and weak pulls keep the current,
initially program, order, so a new edge moves the nodes it genuinely
pulls instead of whole branches. The canvas also freezes the layout
frame (scales/origin) while agent beats are playing, re-framing once
the burst settles.

Tests: Test_GraphLayoutStability (damped vs plain, strong pull,
append), Test_CanvasGraphFold (badges, parts, anonymous products).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

eb6423eba568e407f5a09f6fac9faf20073f1e52Matthew Hammer2026-09-03T19:44:39-06:002026-09-03T19:44:39-06:00Correct the missing-primitive count: 53, not 35

An earlier reading of a wrapped build log undercounted. Measured against the
generated module, 53 primitives are unimplemented -- 27 zarith, 19 Base, 2
bin_prot, 2 expect_test_collector, 3 other.

This changes the conclusion in one respect: passing the existing JS runtimes
via (wasm_of_ocaml (javascript_files ...)) made essentially no difference
(54 to 53), rather than clearing the non-zarith stubs as previously
recorded. The blocker is therefore broader than zarith alone -- it is
bignum's whole Jane Street Core dependency -- which makes replacing Bigint
with a JS BigInt backend more attractive than porting zarith, since it drops
both at once.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

1befbd45734e89d18de39533c5141a0babf760acMatthew Hammer2026-09-03T19:42:54-06:002026-09-03T19:42:54-06:00Move DOM utilities out of util; wasm build blocked on zarith

Making the wasm build get further meant getting js_of_ocaml's DOM bindings
out of the evaluator's dependency cone entirely. JsUtil, API, OpenRouter and
FloatingElement move to util_web; a small Clock keeps the two time
primitives [language] actually needs. Web-side directories now uniformly
`open Util_web` (which re-exports Util), so moving a module between the two
libraries no longer causes churn at the call sites.

That also surfaced real js_of_ocaml 5 -> 6 portability debt, since the two
versions disagree: Firebug exists only in 5.x and Console only in 6.x,
setTimeout changed from (Js.callback, Js.number_t) to (Js.meth_callback,
float), and scrollTop/Date/performance now return Js.number_t where they
returned float or int. The surviving uses go through Js.Unsafe so one source
compiles under both.

Result: the Wasm module builds and loads, then fails at link on missing
primitives, about thirty of them ml_z_* -- zarith. Hazel's Int and Nat are
arbitrary-precision and language reaches Bigint from six files including
BuiltinsBase, so this is load-bearing for the semantics, not droppable for a
benchmark. Passing the existing JS runtimes via (wasm_of_ocaml
(javascript_files ...)) clears bin_prot, expect_test_collector and most
Base_* stubs, but zarith has only a hand-written JS runtime and it does not
port.

Verified: dune build src bench --profile release clean on the ordinary
switch, @test-quick 3457 tests passing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

d954eaebbf57bb6bdeea8a4655a02494ab8c54d4disconcision2026-09-03T18:30:34-07:002026-09-03T18:30:34-07:00canvas: fix blank start, attach tool beats to content, stagger arrivals

From the first live-run journal: the turn-start avatar reused a site
remembered from the PREVIOUS slide (off the new blank board), so the
camera glided to nothing. Avatar/camera state now resets per slide and
a remembered site must lie on the board.

Tool snapshots are captured before statics run (CachedStatics.empty),
so labeled beats were blank and the content arrived one beat later,
unlabeled. Pending beats now attach to the next distinct live state
(label + avatar kept, held at most 1.5s). Unlabeled states whose graph
delta is zero (statics identity churn on eval ticks, ~3Hz) refresh the
tail in place instead of queueing a beat.

Arrival choreography for agent beats: new elements grow in one by one
(320ms apart, span capped 2.4s), existing elements wait for the
arrivals and then relayout slowly (550ms). Dwell tracks the stagger.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

9eb1c36791c9b203d77844f6e89015b0f461c2dbMatthew Hammer2026-09-03T19:26:10-06:002026-09-03T19:26:10-06:00Make the wasm switch setup actually solvable

Four separate things had to be true at once, none obvious:

  - add the archive opam repo (as make deps does), the only source of
    ppx_deriving_qcheck.0.6, which is in turn the only version compatible
    with ppxlib 0.35;
  - pin every shared package to what the main switch resolved to, since a
    free solve walks the Jane Street packages back toward OCaml 4.10 and
    reports a spurious conflict;
  - drop bisect_ppx, which is only needed under --instrument-with and
    unconstrained pulls a pre-2.x release capping ocaml < 5.0;
  - document the cmake/ninja depext, which comes from binaryen-bin via
    wasm_of_ocaml and needs sudo.

Also fail fast with a clear message when cmake or ninja is missing, rather
than after opam has already built the compiler.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

c6328f832fe19d9ad463880397824dfbef9a41c5Matthew Hammer2026-09-03T19:01:44-06:002026-09-03T19:01:44-06:00Pin the wasm switch to js_of_ocaml 6.2.0

js_of_ocaml-compiler >= 6.3.0 requires cmdliner >= 2.0. Hazel pins
uuidm = 0.9.8, which builds with topkg and so caps cmdliner < 2.0, making
6.4.1 unsolvable. 6.2.0 needs only cmdliner >= 1.1.0 and keeps the uuidm pin
intact; both backends still come from a single compiler version, which is
what the comparison requires.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

b9b82cf013820bcf64b59b8c29ae81e9e1f5ef25Matthew Hammer2026-09-03T18:45:10-06:002026-09-03T18:45:10-06:00Isolate the wasm bench run from the shared build tree

run.sh now builds into _build-wasm rather than _build, so a run under the
wasm switch does not invalidate the ordinary switch's artifacts, and warns
when invoked from the primary checkout instead of a linked worktree. Both
matter because the run rewrites bench/wasm/dune in place.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

e265f07e06ec5782dbefe0dcb81b663149c69ecfMatthew Hammer2026-09-03T18:40:00-06:002026-09-03T18:40:00-06:00Drop the two livelit elaboration tests

Both assert behaviour that the builtin livelits provided: `^slider(50)`
elaborating to `50`, and `^emotion(50)` leaving elaboration ids in the
statics map. With livelits out of the language core, `^slider` is unbound
and the application stays unexpanded.

The `^^slider` tests exercise the projector, a separate mechanism, and are
untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

cb12d98b7d751dc6a2f8d7170cb32b55513ddbc7Matthew Hammer2026-09-03T18:37:46-06:002026-09-03T18:37:46-06:00SPIKE: Bonsai-free evaluator benchmark for wasm_of_ocaml

virtual_dom v0.16 requires js_of_ocaml < 6.0.0 and wasm_of_ocaml is only
released from 6.0.1, so the two cannot coexist in one opam switch. Anything
in the Bonsai cone therefore cannot be compiled to Wasm at all, and making
[language] Bonsai-free is a precondition for measuring the evaluator.

Split [util] into [util] (pure + js_of_ocaml only) and [util_web]
(Bonsai/Virtual_dom). Util_web re-exports all of Util, so most consumers
only changed `open Util` to `open Util_web`. JsUtil and Key were each split
rather than moved, keeping their js_of_ocaml-only majority in [util]; that
alone avoided ~48 files of churn.

Drop livelits from the language core: they were the last thing holding
Virtual_dom, since the three builtin livelits construct vdom directly.
LivelitCtx keeps its types so Ctx.LivelitEntry still typechecks, but nothing
populates it and LivelitProj renders a placeholder. This part is NOT
mergeable -- the principled version registers livelit views from the web
layer.

Add bench/wasm, which links only [language] + [menhirParser] and parses via
menhirParser rather than the haz3lcore zipper path. It emits the JSON shape
bench/compare.js already consumes. setup-switch.sh builds the Bonsai-free
switch; run.sh builds both backends from the same compiler version and runs
them on node 22 (Wasm GC postdates the node 18 this repo otherwise uses).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

4e43dfb7a2248ea5c4544cc070eeea575bc8e756disconcision2026-09-03T17:31:07-07:002026-09-03T17:31:07-07:00Merge quiver-assist into artifact-grout: one ownership list for Tab and the quiver

Brings quiver-assist's merge of completion-provenance (review rounds,
CompletionQuery split, strategy header, severance/arms fixes, Codex
fixes, the ownership-once Tab/quiver design) into this branch's
structure (tab-slice, grout-free edit state, GroutPlace):

- CompletionQuery is THE zipper-facing home; tab_slice and the
  display-slicing tab_text move in from CanonicalCompletion, and
  tab_action takes ~display/~marks so Tab still accepts what is on
  screen. CodeEditable dispatches tab_action; QuiverDec draws the
  owned list at the caret (QuiverLayout).
- Hotspot files keep this branch's versions per the merge map:
  Indentation (derived-hole continuation arm), Relatives (grout-free
  regrout = strip), PersistentZipper (strip on load), Test_Editing
  pins re-pinned grout-free with completion-provenance's put-down
  spacing; PendingDelim pins grout-free; closer-severance pins to this
  branch's hole padding; the Tab expectation after `then 4` is
  `" else  "` (tab-slice materializes the display pads around the
  unfilled hole — the design doc's flagged choice).
- Saves derive holes (GroutPlace) before printing and print them as ¿:
  the pin printer wraps terms by the segment's skel, which fails on a
  hole-less fragment (`^^probe(x);`) and silently dropped ^^probe
  wrappers from this branch's grout-free saves — the #2450 data loss
  upstream fixed by regrouting on load, which this edit state cannot
  do. Derived holes as markers keep saves fast-parseable (a body-less
  `in` before `))` is a menhir error without one) and every save a
  fixed point; the reader already destructs and strips markers.
- The completion wrapper drops the ~fuel passthrough completion-
  provenance removed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

72fea6a037da69de5fb77d460d1ba722d90bedcddisconcision2026-09-03T17:08:29-07:002026-09-03T17:08:29-07:00canvas: camera follow + beat choreography

CanvasCamera owns scroll+zoom (viewport center + zoom; fit and follow
are both camera animations). New `follow` toggle (canvas_follow, default
on): an avatar hop outside a central dead zone glides the camera in the
avatar's own 450ms, framing the sites touched this burst with hysteresis
(replaces the zoom-out-only burst auto-fit; manual wheel zoom pins zoom).
Avatar now embodied from the first thinking token (viewport center when
nothing has resolved yet), so the reasoning bubble shows on turn one.
Beats: dwell weighted by graph delta, compressed elastically before any
coalescing (cap 4->8); graph elements move 260ms after the avatar sets
off (Animation delay + fill backwards); toast lands in the settle phase.
Console testers __canvasCameraTo / __canvasFollowTo.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

